---
import { getYearsOfExperience } from "@/consts";
---

<article class="max-w-2xl space-y-5 text-pretty">
  <p>
    I'm a full-stack engineer in Santo Domingo with <span id="years-exp-about"
      >{getYearsOfExperience()}</span
    >+ years of professional experience. Most of my work is TypeScript: Vue 3 and React on the
    front, Node.js and NestJS behind them, and the cloud or servers they run on. More and more of it involves LLMs, and that has
    taught me as much about where not to use a model as where to use one.
  </p>

  <p>
    My rule is that the model reads and the code decides. A bug in date arithmetic can be found
    and fixed. A model that decided a document was "expired" for reasons it can't show you can't
    be. I also don't trust a model's confidence score until I've measured it. In my own pipeline
    it was 1.00 on every field, including the one it got wrong.
  </p>

  <p>
    A lot of my time goes into code other people wrote. Before refactoring a government system's
    frontend service layer, I measured it first: 27 service files, ~222 uses of <code>any</code>, no tests. Then I replaced the pattern without breaking the modules that still used the old
    one. I also onboard and mentor the engineers who join my project at Thryv.
  </p>
</article>

<script>
  import { getYearsOfExperience } from "@/consts";

  document.addEventListener("astro:page-load", () => {
    const yearsSpan = document.getElementById("years-exp-about");
    if (yearsSpan) {
      yearsSpan.textContent = getYearsOfExperience().toString();
    }
  });
</script>
